- id: C:behaviour/vulture.core.vulture-state
  type: behaviour
  name: A new scanner
  summary: Vulture() starts with empty lists of definitions per kind, an empty set of used names, and exit code
    NoDeadCode.
  stance: observed
  of:
  - C:interface/vulture.core.Vulture
  - C:interface/vulture.core.Vulture.__init__
  - C:component/vulture.core
  when: Vulture(verbose=False, ignore_names=None, ignore_decorators=None) is made
  does: 'Keeps verbose; makes eight utils.LoggingList collections, each knowing its kind: defined_attrs ("attribute"),
    defined_classes ("class"), defined_funcs ("function"), defined_imports ("import"), defined_methods ("method"),
    defined_props ("property"), defined_vars ("variable"), unreachable_code ("unreachable_code"); used_names is
    a utils.LoggingSet("name"). ignore_names and ignore_decorators default to empty lists. filename starts as Path(),
    code as [], exit_code as ExitCode.NoDeadCode, noqa_lines as {}. A Reachability is made whose report function
    defines an item in unreachable_code with confidence 100. One Vulture is used for many scans: the definitions
    and used names of every file scanned accumulate, so a name used in one file makes a definition of the same name
    in any file used. The private _log(self, *args, file=None, force=False) prints its arguments (like print) to
    file, standard output by default, only when verbose or force; if printing raises UnicodeEncodeError it prints
    the bytes of the arguments joined by spaces (" ".join(map(str, args)).encode()) instead.'
  edges:
  - given: Vulture(verbose=True)._log("日本人À")
    then: prints 日本人À and a newline
  span:
    file: vulture/core.py
    lines: 190-225
  provenance:
