- id: C:behaviour/vulture.core.define-item
  type: behaviour
  name: Recording a definition, unless it is ignored
  summary: Every definition goes through the private _define, which drops it when an ignore rule, an --ignore-names
    pattern or a noqa comment covers it.
  stance: observed
  of:
  - C:interface/vulture.core.Vulture.visit_arg
  - C:interface/vulture.core.Vulture.visit_Attribute
  when: a visitor finds a definition; the private _define(self, collection, name, first_node, last_node=None, message="",
    confidence=DEFAULT_CONFIDENCE, ignore=None) is called
  does: 'typ is the collection''s kind; first_lineno is lines.get_first_line_number(first_node) (the first decorator''s
    line for a decorated definition); last_lineno is lines.get_last_line_number(last_node or first_node). The definition
    is ignored when (a) the kind''s ignore function says so for (self.filename, name), or (b) the name matches one
    of ignore_names with fnmatch.fnmatchcase (case-sensitive glob over the whole name), or (c) noqa.ignore_line(self.noqa_lines,
    first_lineno, ERROR_CODES[typ]) is true (a noqa comment on the first line naming that kind''s code, or a bare
    noqa). Ignored: logs ''Ignoring <typ> "<name>"'' (verbose). Else appends Item(name, typ, self.filename, first_lineno,
    last_lineno, message, confidence) to the collection. The private _define_variable(self, name, node, confidence=DEFAULT_CONFIDENCE)
    defines into defined_vars with the variable ignore rule. visit_arg defines each function argument (node.arg)
    as a variable with confidence 100. visit_Attribute: an attribute being stored (obj.x = ...) defines an attribute
    x (confidence 60, no ignore rule beyond names and noqa); an attribute being read adds x to used_names; deleting
    does neither.'
  edges:
  - given: 'def foo(x, y): return x + 1'
    then: y is an unused variable with confidence 100; x is used
  - given: A._a = 1
    then: an unused attribute _a (attributes have no underscore rule)
  span:
    file: vulture/core.py
    lines: 429-485
  provenance:
    mode: derived
    sources:
    - vulture/core.py#L429-L485
    confidence: 0.9
