- id: C:behaviour/vulture.core.format-strings-with-locals
  type: behaviour
  name: Names used through format strings applied to locals()
  summary: A %-format or str.format string applied to locals() uses the names in its fields; getattr and hasattr
    with a string literal use that attribute name.
  stance: observed
  of:
  - C:interface/vulture.core.Vulture.visit_BinOp
  - C:interface/vulture.core.Vulture.visit_Call
  when: the visitor meets a binary operation or a call
  does: 'visit_BinOp: when the left side is a string literal (utils.is_ast_string), the operator is % and the right
    side is exactly locals() (a call of the name locals with no arguments or keywords; the private staticmethod
    _is_locals_call(node)), every name matched by the regular expression %\((\w+)\) in the string is used. visit_Call:
    (1) a call of the plain name getattr with 2 or 3 positional arguments, or hasattr with exactly 2, whose second
    argument is a string literal, uses that string. (2) A call "<string literal>.format(...)" with a keyword argument
    **locals() is handed to the private _handle_new_format_string(self, s): string.Formatter().parse gives the field
    names (a malformed string raises ValueError and gives no names); for each field name, bracketed parts matching
    \[\w*\] are removed and the rest split at "."; each part that starts like an identifier (re.match of [a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*)
    is used. Names inside f-strings are ordinary Name nodes and are used by visit_Name. Neither visitor recurses
    itself; the generic visit goes on into the children.'
  edges:
  - given: '''%(a)s, %(b)d'' % locals()'
    then: a and b used (and locals, as a Name)
  - given: '''{a.b}, {c.d.e} {f[g]} {h[i][j].k}''.format(**locals())'
    then: a b c d e f h k used
  - given: '''{0.b}''.format(**locals())'
    then: b used; 0 is not an identifier
  - given: '"{} {a}".format(1, a=used_var)'
    then: nothing from the string
  - given: getattr(Thing, "x", 1, 2) or hasattr(Thing, "x", None)
    then: x not used
  span:
    file: vulture/core.py
    lines: 487-551
  provenance:
    mode: derived
    sources:
    - vulture/core.py#L487-L551
    confidence: 0.9
