- id: C:behaviour/vulture.core.import-aliases
  type: behaviour
  name: Defining imported names
  summary: Each name an import binds is an import definition with confidence 90; an alias also uses the original
    name.
  stance: observed
  of:
  - C:interface/vulture.core.Vulture.visit_Import
  - C:interface/vulture.core.Vulture.visit_ImportFrom
  when: the private _add_aliases(self, node) is called for an Import or ImportFrom node (the visitors are in lines
    601-606)
  does: 'For each alias in the statement, in order: the name is the first dotted part of the imported name ("os.path"
    gives "os"); the definition is the alias when there is one, else that name; it is defined as an import with
    confidence 90 and the import ignore rule (star imports and every import in an __init__.py are never recorded);
    the item spans the whole import statement''s lines. When there is an alias, the full original name (name_and_alias.name,
    e.g. "foo" or "a.b") is added to used_names.'
  edges:
  - given: import foo as bar
    then: defines bar; uses foo
  - given: import os.path
    then: defines os
