- id: C:behaviour/vulture.core.visit-order
  type: behaviour
  name: How the tree is walked
  summary: The visitor walks every node children first, then lets the reachability analysis see the node, then runs
    the node's own visitor, then parses and walks its type comment.
  stance: observed
  of:
  - C:interface/vulture.core.Vulture.visit
  - C:interface/vulture.core.Vulture.generic_visit
  when: visit(node) is called on the module tree (by scan) and recursively
  does: 'visit(node): 1. generic_visit(node) first: for every field of the node, in field order, visits each AST
    element of a list field and an AST value of a single field (so children before parents, which lets the reachability
    analysis work bottom-up). 2. self.reachability.visit(node). 3. Looks up a method named "visit_" + the node''s
    class name; with verbose, logs the node''s line number (lineno, or 1), ast.dump(node) and that source line (self.code[lineno
    - 1], or "" when there is no code). 4. Calls that method if there is one. 5. When the node has a type_comment
    that is not None, parses it with ast.parse(type_comment, filename="<type_comment>", mode="func_type" for FunctionDef
    and AsyncFunctionDef, "eval" otherwise) and visits that tree too, so names in type comments are used; a type
    comment that does not parse raises SyntaxError, which scan catches. generic_visit never calls a visit_ method
    itself; visitors other than visit do not recurse.'
  edges:
  - given: 'some_var = {}  # type: Dict[str, str]'
    then: Dict and str used
  - given: 'def bad(): with "# type: bogus"'
    then: SyntaxError, scan sets InvalidInput
  span:
    file: vulture/core.py
    lines: 628-664
  provenance:
    mode: derived
    sources:
    - vulture/core.py#L628-L664
    confidence: 0.9
    state: known
