- id: C:behaviour/vulture.noqa.parse-noqa
  type: behaviour
  name: Finding noqa comments in a module
  summary: parse_noqa maps each error code to the set of line numbers whose noqa comment names it, with "all" for
    a bare noqa.
  stance: observed
  of:
  - C:interface/vulture.noqa.parse_noqa
  - C:interface/vulture.noqa._parse_error_codes
  - C:component/vulture.noqa
  when: parse_noqa(code) is called with the module's lines (a list of strings, as scan passes code.splitlines())
  does: 'Starts a defaultdict(set). For each line, numbered from 1, searches NOQA_REGEXP anywhere in the line (re.search;
    so the text "# noqa" inside a string literal counts too). On a match, _parse_error_codes(match) takes the named
    group "codes"; when it is None (a bare "# noqa", or text after it that is not a code list) the codes are ["all"];
    otherwise the group is split at "," and each part stripped of surrounding whitespace (the group may end with
    a trailing separator, giving codes as written: "V101, V106" gives ["V101", "V106"]). Each code is mapped through
    NOQA_CODE_MAP (F401 becomes V104, F841 becomes V107; other codes stay as written, in the case written) and the
    line number is added to that code''s set. Returns the mapping.'
  edges:
  - given: '["import this  # noqa: F401"]'
    then: '{"V104": {1}}'
  - given: '["x = 1  # noqa"]'
    then: '{"all": {1}}'
  - given: '["# NoQA: V101,      V103, \t V104"]'
    then: '{"V101": {1}, "V103": {1}, "V104": {1}}'
  - given: '["#noqa"] or ["# Hello, noqa"]'
    then: '{} (the pattern needs "# noqa" with exactly one space)'
  span:
    file: vulture/noqa.py
    lines: 1-39
  provenance:
    mode: derived
    sources:
    - vulture/noqa.py#L1-L39
    confidence: 0.9
    state: known
  made:
    move: derive
    run: 2026-11-05-2316-661d
    step: 1
    node: '1'
    engine: model:hand-g1c
  ruling:
  - is: kept
