- id: C:behaviour/vulture.reachability.if-statements
  type: behaviour
  name: if statements with constant or ending branches
  summary: An if whose test is constant reports the branch that can never run; an if whose every branch stops is
    marked as not falling through.
  stance: observed
  of:
  - C:interface/vulture.reachability.Reachability.visit
  when: the private _handle_reachability_if(self, node) runs for an If node (an elif is an If inside the orelse
    of another)
  does: 'Always false (utils.condition_is_always_false(test)): reports name "if" from the If node (its test line)
    to the last statement of its body, message "unsatisfiable ''if'' condition"; the body is not analysed; the if
    part counts as falling through; the else part is analysed (see below). Always true: the body is analysed (reports
    inside it come first); the else part is analysed; then, when there is an else, reports name "else" from its
    first statement to its last, message "unreachable ''else'' block" (the else line itself is not counted; for
    an elif chain the orelse''s first statement is the inner If, whose line is the elif line); without an else,
    reports name "if" for the whole if statement (first and last node the If itself), message "redundant if-condition".
    Otherwise the body and the else part are analysed. The else part (private _can_else_fall_through(self, or_else,
    condition_always_true)): when there is no else it falls through unless the condition is always true; when there
    is one it is the result of analysing it. When neither the if part nor the else part can fall through, the If
    is marked as not falling through, so the statements after it are reported as "unreachable code after ''if''".'
  edges:
  - given: "if False:\n    pass"
    then: (1, 2, if) unsatisfiable 'if' condition
  - given: "if True:\n    a = 1\n    b = 2\nelse:\n    c = 3\n    d = 3"
    then: (5, 2, else) unreachable 'else' block
  - given: "if [5]:\n    pass"
    then: (1, 2, if) redundant if-condition
  - given: "def foo(a):\n    if True:\n        return 0\n    print(\":-(\")"
    then: '[(2, 2, if) redundant if-condition, (4, 1, if) unreachable code after ''if'']'
  - given: if/elif/else all returning, then a statement
    then: 'the statement: unreachable code after ''if'''
  - given: an if with no else whose body returns
    then: 'falls through: nothing after it is reported'
  span:
    file: vulture/reachability.py
    lines: 64-119
  provenance:
    mode: derived
    sources:
    - vulture/reachability.py#L64-L119
    confidence: 0.9
    state: known
  made:
    move: derive
    run: 2026-11-05-2316-661d
    step: 1
    node: '1'
    engine: model:hand-g1c
