- id: C:behaviour/vulture.reachability.try-statements
  type: behaviour
  name: try statements
  summary: A try whose body and every handler stop is marked as not falling through; an else after a body that always
    stops is reported.
  stance: observed
  of:
  - C:interface/vulture.reachability.Reachability.visit
  when: the private _handle_reachability_try(self, node) runs for a Try
  does: Analyses the try body. When the body cannot fall through and there is an else, reports name "else" over
    the else block, message "unreachable 'else' block". Then analyses the handlers' bodies in order until one can
    fall through (any() stops there, so the handlers after the first falling-through one are not analysed and unreachable
    code inside them is not reported). When neither the body nor any handler can fall through, marks the Try as
    not falling through. The finally block is not considered, and a try whose body falls through does not look at
    its else. (ast.TryStar, except*, is not handled.)
  edges:
  - given: 'try: return 2 / except IndexError: raise / except Exception: raise / then print'
    then: 'the print: unreachable code after ''try'''
  - given: 'try: raise / except: return 1 / else: print'
    then: 'the else body: unreachable ''else'' block'
  - given: 'try: print / except: return 1 / else: print'
    then: nothing
  span:
    file: vulture/reachability.py
    lines: 194-220
  provenance:
    mode: derived
    sources:
    - vulture/reachability.py#L194-L220
    confidence: 0.9
