- id: C:behaviour/vulture.utils.constant-conditions
  type: behaviour
  name: Deciding whether a condition is constant
  summary: condition_is_always_false and condition_is_always_true evaluate a test expression safely, treating anything
    that is not a literal as unknown.
  stance: observed
  of:
  - C:interface/vulture.utils.condition_is_always_false
  - C:interface/vulture.utils.condition_is_always_true
  when: the reachability analysis asks about the test of an if, elif, while or conditional expression
  does: 'Both use the private _safe_eval(node, default), which never runs code: for "and" it evaluates every operand
    and returns all() of the results, for "or" any(); for "not x" it returns not _safe_eval(x, not default); for
    anything else it returns ast.literal_eval(node) (literals, containers of literals: truthiness then decides)
    or, when that raises ValueError (a name, call, attribute, comparison...), default. condition_is_always_false(c)
    is not _safe_eval(c, True) (unknowns count as true, so only a certainly falsy expression is false); condition_is_always_true(c)
    is _safe_eval(c, False) taken as a truth value.'
  edges:
  - given: False, None, 0, "", [], {}
    then: always false
  - given: 'True, 2, "s", [''foo'', ''bar''], {''a'': 1, ''b'': 2}'
    then: always true
  - given: foo and False
    then: always false
  - given: foo or True
    then: always true
  - given: not foo
    then: neither
  - given: __name__ == "__main__", chr(-1), globals(), ().__class__
    then: neither (never evaluated)
  span:
    file: vulture/utils.py
    lines: 19-53
  provenance:
    mode: derived
    sources:
    - vulture/utils.py#L19-L53
    confidence: 0.9
