- id: C:behaviour/vulture.whitelists.whitelist_utils.whitelist-object
  type: behaviour
  name: An object whose every attribute exists
  summary: Whitelist() returns None for any attribute read, so whitelist files can use names on it and still run.
  stance: observed
  of:
  - C:interface/vulture.whitelists.whitelist_utils.Whitelist
  - C:interface/vulture.whitelists.whitelist_utils.Whitelist.__getattr__
  - C:component/vulture.whitelists.whitelist_utils
  when: a whitelist file reads an attribute of a Whitelist instance (w = Whitelist(); w.visit_Assign)
  does: 'Whitelist is a plain class whose __getattr__(self, _) returns None for any attribute that is not found
    normally, so any attribute read succeeds without error. The module ends with "assert Whitelist" (so the class
    itself is used). Its docstring tells how whitelists work: run "vulture myscript.py mydir mywhitelist.py" with
    a Python file that uses the falsely reported names; a whitelist must not import modules such as sys, because
    that would hide unused imports of them in other files.'
  edges:
  - given: Whitelist().anything
    then: None
  span:
