- id: N:decision/whitelists-preferred
  type: decision
  name: Whitelists over noqa and ignore options
  summary: The recommended way to silence false positives is a whitelist module, not noqa comments or --ignore-names
    / --ignore-decorators.
  stance: observed
  chose: whitelist Python modules scanned with the code (and bundled ones for standard modules, scanned when imported)
  over:
  - option: noqa comments
    because: they add visual noise to the code and make it harder to read
  - option: --ignore-names and --ignore-decorators
    because: whitelists are checked for syntax when scanned, and can be run to check that whitelisted code still
      exists
  because: whitelists are real Python and can be checked
